00:00:54,870 --> 00:01:02,910
Speaker 3: truly the godmother of East Bay progressive politics. The former City of Berkeley vice mayor and eight-term council member was born and

6
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:10,050
Speaker 2: raised in Jefferson, Arkansas. As the granddaughter of slaves, she was passionate about justice and civil rights.

7
00:01:10,180 --> 00:01:37,400
Speaker 2: After moving to Berkeley in the 1940s, she became active in the anti-war movement, fought on behalf of unions, advocated for HIV and AIDS awareness, care, and treatment, and helped organize the free Mandela movement. She was also the first elected official in the United States to advocate for needle exchange programs. During her tenure as a Berkeley elected official, she was instrumental in creating multiple city commissions, including the Berkeley Commission on Labor.

8
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:49,990
