Speaker 2: the Berkeley Marina together, where she talked to me about acupuncture and natural remedies like cayenne pepper and warm water for colds and the importance of exercise. Maudel was a woman of great faith. During the '70s, we enjoyed attending the church for tomorrow, which formerly was the church for today. We went there together.

15
00:03:08,770 --> 00:03:16,920
Speaker 2: And this is where I realized that her passion for service and justice was driven by her commitment to what she called doing the Lord's work on this earth.

16
00:03:17,090 --> 00:03:17,460
She was a

17
00:03:17,470 --> 00:03:38,450
Speaker 2: woman who understands that she had to have a comprehensive agenda. It just couldn't be a single issue like health care, seniors, or peace and justice. But it had to be about being committed to comprehensive and positive changes that seek to improve the lives of all Americans. Maudel worked at the Berkeley Co-op Credit Union. She engaged all of us in the 70s, mind you, in financial

18
00:03:38,650 --> 00:03:56,230
Speaker 2: literacy and urged me as a young single student to buy a house because she reminded me over and over again that one's equity in one's home was the primary path to the middle class and that that was the main way that I could get the resources to take care of my kids and send them to school,

